s\n\nSum graph\n\n;\n\nSpum\n\n;\n\nSum-diameter\n\n;\n\nGraph labeling\n\n;\n\nPath\n\n  * Previous article in this issue\n  * Next article in this issue\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn 1990, Harary [2] defined the sum graph of to be given by and if (see for example, Fig. 1). Not every graph G is a sum graph of some set L; for example, no connected G is a sum graph, because in a sum graph, the vertex with the largest label must be isolated. Yet, Harary showed [2] that any graph G in union with at least a sum number of isolated vertices is a sum graph of some set L. Henceforth, let {I}_{n} be a set of n isolated vertices for .\n\nShortly after, Goodell, Beveridge, Gallagher, Goodwin, Gyori, and Joseph [1] defined the spum of a graph G as the minimum difference between the largest and the smallest labels in L, for which is a sum graph of L. In the same paper, Goodell et al. [1] evaluated . More recently, in 2021, Singla, Tiwari, and Tripathi [5] evaluated and . Our first main result is Theorem 3.26, where we settle a conjecture that was originally proposed by Singla, Tiwari, and Tripathi [5, Conjecture 7.1] and subsequently modified by Li [4, Conjecture 3.4] on , where {P}_{n} is a path of n vertices.\n\nIn 1994, Harary [3] extended the notion of a sum graph; he defined the integral sum graph of to be given by and if . He then defined the integral sum number as the minimum number of vertices for which is an integral sum graph. In 2021, Singla et al. [5] extended the notion of spum; they defined the integral spum of a graph G as the minimum difference between the largest and the smallest labels in L, for which is an integral sum graph of L. In the same paper, Singla et al. [5] evaluated , , and . Our next main result Theorem 4.21 is an improvement on the best known lower bound on in [5].\n\nLast year, Li [4] introduced the more natural sum diameter of a graph G as the minimum difference between the largest and the smallest labels of L, for which is a sum graph of L for any .